  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAn upper level trough is currently digging into the \nnorthwestern/western CONUS with ridging over the Plains into the \nGreat Lakes and northeastern CONUS. The remnants of Hurricane Isaias \nis over the southeastern US and will slowly propagate northeast. At \nthe surface, there is a low over the Northern Plains with surface \ntroughing extending down into the High Plains. Lee troughing has \nincreased, strengthening the pressure gradient over our area, \nleading to breezy southerly winds especially for areas west of the \nKansas Turnpike. These breezy conditions will persist through Monday \nas the upper trough continues to dig into the western CONUS leading \nto an increased pressure gradient from lee troughing remaining over \nour area. Sustained winds will be between 15-25 mph with gusts up to \n35 mph each day especially in central and south-central KS. Well \nabove normal high temperatures are forecast through Tuesday as \nincreased thickness and above normal 850 mb temperatures persist. \nHighs are expected to be in the upper 80s to lower 90s with the \nwarmest days being Monday and Tuesday.   \n\nA pattern change is likely to start on Tuesday afternoon-Tuesday \nnight as the aforementioned upper trough over the western CONUS \npropagates east and approaches the Rockies into Wednesday. This \ntrough will bring the next round of shower and storm chances to the \nregion especially as a cold front is expected to push south through \nthe central Plains into Kansas by Tuesday morning. These rain \nchances will be possible from Tuesday evening to Thursday before the \ntrough continues to slide east on Friday, though the ECMWF has a \nslower solution where it keeps the trough in the Rockies through \nFriday leading to some uncertainty on rain chances into next \nweekend. Strong to severe storms may be possible with this activity \nas effective wind shear increases combined with ample instability \nand forcing from the trough and cold front. Heavy rainfall appears \nlikely as well as PWATs approach 2\" (greater than the maximum for \nthis time of year) across our area. Details on timing, location and \nimpacts will continue to be refined as we get closer. \n\nFrom mid to late week, temperatures are forecast to become more \nseasonable as highs return to the 70s. Moving into next weekend, \nconfidence is very low on temperatures as there is a large spread in \nguidance. The ECMWF depicts upper ridging to be over the Plains with \nhighs in the 60s and lows in the 50s, whereas the GFS has a deep \nupper trough over the region with highs in the 40s and lows in the \n30s or potentially colder... Stay tuned!",
